# Add a test to main.rs

Current state: [main.rs](/work/project/main.rs) only prints `"bonjour"`; there is no `Cargo.toml`, so Rust unit tests cannot run yet.

## Step 1: Make the crate runnable with Cargo

Create a minimal [Cargo.toml](/work/project/Cargo.toml) for a binary crate named after the workspace (e.g. `work`) with `edition = "2021"`, so `cargo test` can discover tests in `main.rs`.

## Step 2: Extract a testable helper in main.rs

In [main.rs](/work/project/main.rs), pull the printed string into a small function and call it from `main`:

```rust
fn greeting() -> &'static str {
    "bonjour"
}

fn main() {
    println!("{}", greeting());
}
```

## Step 3: Add the unit test in main.rs

Append a `#[cfg(test)]` module that asserts `greeting()` returns `"bonjour"`:

```rust
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn greeting_is_bonjour() {
        assert_eq!(greeting(), "bonjour");
    }
}
```

No other files need changes. After approval, verify with `cargo test`.

